{
"dur": 66.475,
"shots": [
{
"i": 1,
"start": 0.0,
"vo_start": 0.6,
"vo_end": 8.08,
"text": "Around 2000, a mobile phone was a luxury in India. In towns and villages, people made calls from yellow STD-PCO booths.",
"end": 8.355
},
{
"i": 2,
"start": 8.355,
"vo_start": 8.63,
"vo_end": 14.621,
"text": "Those booths ran on landlines: expensive to install, messy wiring, constant repairs.",
"end": 14.896
},
{
"i": 3,
"start": 14.896,
"vo_start": 15.171,
"vo_end": 22.571,
"text": "Micromax's idea: a booth phone that worked on a SIM card instead. Put in a SIM, attach an antenna, and the booth is ready.",
"end": 22.846
},
{
"i": 4,
"start": 22.846,
"vo_start": 23.121,
"vo_end": 33.161,
"text": "They sold these wireless booth phones through telecom operators: first Airtel, then others. Every minute of every call made money for the operator, so they loved it.",
"end": 33.436
},
{
"i": 5,
"start": 33.436,
"vo_start": 33.711,
"vo_end": 42.766,
"text": "Before that, the booth phones had one big customer: Nokia, which bought them to supply its own booth business. Revenue reached about 23 crore rupees.",
"end": 43.041
},
{
"i": 6,
"start": 43.041,
"vo_start": 43.316,
"vo_end": 50.956,
"text": "Then, the story goes, Nokia called. It was getting out of the booth business. Micromax had a factory full of phones and no buyer.",
"end": 51.231
},
{
"i": 7,
"start": 51.231,
"vo_start": 51.506,
"vo_end": 53.906,
"text": "So Rahul went straight to the phone companies themselves.",
"end": 54.181
},
{
"i": 8,
"start": 54.181,
"vo_start": 54.456,
"vo_end": 59.537,
"text": "Revenue climbed past 100 crore rupees. For a while, that was the business.",
"end": 59.812
},
{
"i": 9,
"start": 59.812,
"vo_start": 60.087,
"vo_end": 65.525,
"text": "The family bought a new car. His parents finally believed this computer business might actually work.",
"end": 66.475
}
],
